[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 3:04] Total revenue is $480M a year. COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%. Take a look at those lines. Which cost line moved the most, and what does that tell you about where to dig next?

[candidate · 4:34] COGS is clearly the driver. It moved 16 points, from 42% to 58%, while overhead moved 2 and labor was flat. That 16 points on $480M is about $77M of margin — and total margin decline is 18 points, so COGS explains roughly 90% of the problem.

Two points of overhead is about $10M, which rounds out the rest. So labor is a non-issue.

Now, COGS as a percent of revenue rising means cost per unit sold is outpacing price per unit. That could be input cost inflation — green coffee and dairy prices — or waste and spoilage, or a mix shift toward ingredient-heavy drinks, or they've held menu prices flat while costs rose.

Do we have a split of COGS by input — beans, dairy, packaging — and separately, has Brew & Bean changed menu prices at all in the last two years?
